    <string name="session_empty">This session has nothing on screen yet.</string>
    <string name="session_truncated">Older output is not shown — the rest is still on your computer.</string>
    <!--
      The reading face's empty state, which is a different sentence from session_empty:
      "nothing on screen yet" describes a terminal, and this screen stopped being one.
      Here nobody has said anything yet, and that is all it means.
    -->
    <string name="session_nothing_said">Nothing said yet.</string>
    <!--
      The working indicator: an agent is mid-turn, and Perch knows how long it has been
      going (the agent's own record says so). One label, two forms — a duration it
      measured, or just the state when nobody has taught Perch to read this agent.
    -->
    <string name="session_working">working · %1$s</string>
    <!--
      Marks the user's own turns. They are the interruptions on this screen — the
      agent's answers are the body of what is being read — so it is they that need a
      label, not every message.
    -->
    <string name="session_you">You</string>
    <string name="session_input_hint">Type here, then send.</string>
    <string name="session_note_shell">No agent here, just a shell: a line break in what you send runs that line.</string>
    <!--
      What "send" actually did, in both cases. The first real trial found that
      "Sent to the session." reads as "it ran", and the user typed a command, saw
      nothing, and concluded the screen was inert (2026-10-13). Nothing is sent to
      execute: the text goes to the pane's input, and pressing enter is what submits
      it — which the escape hatch deliberately leaves to the user, because in a shell
      a line break runs the line, and we are not going to press Enter inside someone's
      terminal on our own. So the sentence says what happened and what is left to do.
    -->
    <string name="session_terminal">Session screen</string>
    <string name="session_sent_shell">Typed at the prompt. Press enter in the key row to run it.</string>
    <string name="session_sent_agent">Sent to the session.</string>
    <string name="session_sent_blocked">Typed at the dialog. Press enter in the key row to submit it.</string>
    <string name="session_unsent">Text is already waiting in the terminal, so sending will add to it: %1$s</string>
    <string name="session_unsent_title">The terminal already has text</string>
    <string name="session_unsent_body">Its own input line holds:\n\n%1$s\n\nSending %2$s will add it to the end of that and submit the two as one line.</string>
    <string name="session_unsent_send_anyway">Send anyway</string>
    <string name="session_unsent_command_title">The input line holds a command</string>
    <string name="session_unsent_command_body">Its own input line holds a command:\n\n%1$s\n\nSending %2$s will make it the argument of that command — not a message. Press Escape in the terminal first if that is not what you want.</string>
    <string name="session_unsent_cancel">Cancel</string>
    <!--
      A menu — codex's model picker, a slash-command list — is open in this session.
      Typing into one drives it (measured: a sentence sent while the picker was open
      advanced it to the next question), and the computer reports such a session as idle,
      so nothing else can tell. Refusing and pointing at the keys is the honest move.
    -->
    <string name="session_menu">A menu is open in this session, so Perch will not type into it. Open the terminal to press the keys.</string>
    <string name="session_setup_title">This agent is still being set up</string>
    <string name="session_setup_fallback">Finish its setup in the terminal before sending messages.</string>
    <string name="session_setup_open_terminal">Open terminal</string>
    <!-- Shown over the text while the reader has scrolled away from the end. -->
    <string name="session_follow_latest">Jump to latest</string>
    <string name="session_usage">Usage</string>
    <string name="session_usage_title">Usage</string>
    <string name="session_usage_reading">Reading the agent\'s own records…</string>
    <string name="session_usage_nothing_yet">This agent has not written down any usage for this session yet. Its own screen still has the running total.</string>
    <string name="session_usage_not_readable">Perch does not know how to read this agent\'s records yet, so there is nothing to show here. Its own screen still has the running total.</string>
    <string name="session_usage_close">Close</string>
    <!--
      The real terminal: the session's own screen, drawn by an emulator. Opened from the menu, and the
      keys under it are the ones a phone keyboard does not have (Escape, Tab, arrows, Ctrl).
    -->
    <string name="terminal_title">Terminal · %1$s</string>
    <string name="terminal_reconnect">Reconnect</string>
    <!--
      "New terminal": end the shell that is running and start another. Named for what it does to the
      *shell* rather than for what it does to the screen, because the two are different — and the thing a
      person usually wants after a shell has stopped is another one, not the same one again.
    -->
    <string name="terminal_new">New</string>
    <!--
      Shown at the bottom of the terminal while the app is coming back to a shell that is still running.
      A state, not a failure: the shell lives on the computer, so a phone that lost the network gets the
      terminal back rather than an error to act on.
    -->
    <string name="terminal_reconnecting">Reconnecting to your terminal…</string>
    <!--
      The console's own entry to the terminal — a shell on the computer, not a session's screen. The other
      name in this file, `session_terminal` ("Session screen"), is the pane viewer.
    -->
    <string name="console_terminal">Terminal</string>
